1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Hi! In this video, we'll look at how you can use Burp to test for OS command injection vulnerabilities.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:20,000
OS command injection is a vulnerability that enables an attacker to execute arbitrary operating system commands on the server that is running an application.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
This can fully compromise the application and its data.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
In Burp Suite Professional, you can use Burp Scanner to automatically flag potential OS command injection vulnerabilities.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
You can also use the repeater tool in both Professional and Community to manually test for these vulnerabilities.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:45,000
If you'd like to follow along with the demo, you can use the OS command injection simple case lab in our Web Security Academy.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
First of all, we'll look at using Burp Scanner to automatically flag any potential vulnerabilities.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Once the lab has launched, identify a request that you want to investigate.

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:03,000
In this case, as we're using the OS command injection simple case lab, we'll choose the request that checks the stock level.

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Go to the Proxy, HTTP History tab, find the request, right click and choose Do Active Scan.

11
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Once the scan has completed, review the issue activity panel on Burp's dashboard and identify any OS command injection issues that Burp Scanner flags.

12
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Next, we'll take a look at using repeater to manually test for command injection vulnerabilities.

13
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:45,000
In the Proxy, HTTP History tab, right click the request you want to investigate and select Send to Repeater.

14
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
We'll use the same request as we just used for the scan.

15
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Change the parameter you want to test to an OS command injection proof of concept attack.

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Here, we'll change the parameters to try and cause the Whoami command to be executed.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
We can try each one in turn and just repeat the process for all parameters in the request.

18
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Once we've updated the parameter, we can hit Send and then review the response to see if the command has actually been executed.

19
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:21,000
In this case, we can see that the store ID parameter is vulnerable.

